<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ezra</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">5th century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/5th-century-bce">5th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ezra was a Jewish priest and scribe who, according to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew-bible">Hebrew Bible</a>, came from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonia">Babylonia</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> in the Persian period with authority from the Achaemenid king Artaxerxes. He is credited with reading the law of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moses">Moses</a> to the people and enforcing it, including the dissolution of marriages with foreign wives. Jewish tradition regards him as a restorer of the Torah, and the biblical Book of Ezra bears his name.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ezra.jpg" alt="Ezra" width="320" height="404" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gustave Doré · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:109.Ezra_Reads_the_Law_to_the_People.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After the Babylonian exile, Aramaic became the everyday language of many Jews, and parts of the books of Daniel and <b>Ezra</b> were written in it.</blockquote><a href="/language/aramaic">Aramaic · Speakers &amp; Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>John Rayner suggested that it was written in Spain in the thirteenth or fourteenth century, since it is missing from the prayer book Sefer Abudarham of about 1340, and Reuven Kimelman thinks Abraham ibn <b>Ezra</b> the likeliest author, from its style and its first appearance in Western Europe.</blockquote><a href="/songs/adon-olam">Adon Olam</a></li><li><blockquote>In the 20th century <b>Ezra</b> Pound translated parts of the song in Canto 6 of The Cantos and returned to its image in Canto 117.</blockquote><a href="/songs/can-vei-la-lauzeta-mover">Can vei la lauzeta mover</a></li><li><blockquote>It has been recorded by the Hilliard Ensemble, Richard Thompson, The Futureheads and others, and parodied by <b>Ezra</b> Pound, Peter Schickele and A. Y. Campbell.</blockquote><a href="/songs/sumer-is-icumen-in">Sumer is icumen in</a></li><li><blockquote>Sergeant <b>Ezra</b> Lee steered David Bushnell's one-man submarine Turtle towards HMS Eagle in New York Harbor.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/september-7">7 September in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Nehemiah, a court official, rebuilt Jerusalem's walls c. 445 BCE, and <b>Ezra</b> brought the law of Moses to the centre of community life.</blockquote><a href="/state/yehud-medinata">Yehud Medinata</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/iraq">Iraq</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judaea">Judea</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/palestine">Palestine</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=32.52,44.42&amp;year=-435" aria-label="Babylon on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5690 981 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Ezra"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6140 1251h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6140 1251h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=32.52,44.42&amp;year=-435">What was here in 435 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;O my God, I am ashamed and blush to lift up my face to thee, my God: for our iniquities are increased over our head, and our trespass is grown up unto the heavens.&#8221;</p><footer>Ezra 9:6, his prayer at the evening sacrifice &#183; King James Version, 1611 &#183; as the Book of Ezra gives his words &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Bible_(King_James)/Ezra" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 539 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
